## Why This Is Asked

Tests critical authorization paths, data-loss prevention, and audit integrity in multi-tenant setups, plus resilience to flaky services and retention policies.

## Key Concepts

- RBAC and DLP enforcement across tenants
- Tamper-evident audit logs (sequencing, signatures/HMAC)
- End-to-end data export redaction
- Seeds and mocks for deterministic, isolated tests
- Flaky service handling and time-based retention checks

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo end-to-end test sketch (Playwright/Cypress-agnostic)
async function testRBACAndDLP() {
  const t = seedTenant('tenantA');
  const uOwner = seedUser(t, 'owner');
  const uEditor = seedUser(t, 'editor');
  const uAuditor = seedUser(t, 'auditor');
  const admin = seedUser(t, 'admin');

  // Owner creates a share with PII
  await login(uOwner);
  await createShare({ tenant: t.id, data: 'PII' });

  // Editor attempts restricted action
  await login(uEditor);
  assertAccessDenied(editShare(t.id));

  // Auditor reads audit trail
  await login(uAuditor);
  assertAuditIncludes(t.id, 'READ', uAuditor.id);

  // Admin exports with redaction
  await login(admin);
  const exportPayload = await exportData(t.id);
  assertRedacts(exportPayload, ['PII']);
  // Verify tamper-evident log
  assertTamperEvidence(t.id);
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Offline-first reliability with service workers is a critical requirement in modern e-commerce applications. This question evaluates whether a candidate understands how to test caching behavior, connectivity state changes, and retry logic in end-to-end tests.

## Key Concepts
- Offline-first UX patterns with service workers
- Network throttling and failure simulation (Cypress or Playwright network interception)
- Test data isolation with fixtures or unique identifiers
- Idempotent order placement to prevent duplicate charges

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: Intercept network requests to simulate offline behavior
cy.intercept('POST', '/api/payment', (req) => {
  req.reply({ statusCode: 503, body: { error: 'Network error' } });
}).as('paymentRequest');

// Simulate going offline
cy.networkIdle(); // or use Cypress offline plugin
cy.window().then((win) => {
  win.navigator.onLine = false;
});

// Verify offline UI and localStorage persistence
cy.get('[data-testid=offline-banner]').should('be.visible');
cy.getLocalStorage('cart').should('exist');

// Restore network and retry
cy.networkOn();
cy.wait('@paymentRequest').then(() => {
  cy.get('[data-testid=order-confirmation]').should('be.visible');
});

// Verify idempotent order creation
cy.get('@paymentRequest').its('response.statusCode').should('eq', 200);
```

## Why Asked
Interview context: Tests understanding of scalable test infrastructure design and architectural trade-offs in enterprise testing environments.

## Key Concepts
Core knowledge: WebDriver for single browser automation, Grid's hub-node topology for distributed testing, session management, resource allocation, parallel execution strategies.

## Architecture Deep Dive
Selenium Grid uses a centralized hub that manages test sessions and routes requests to distributed nodes. The hub acts as a load balancer and session manager, while nodes register their capabilities (browser types, versions, platforms) and execute actual browser sessions. Each node can handle multiple concurrent sessions based on available resources, typically 4-6 sessions per 8GB RAM.

## Large-Scale Use Cases
Netflix runs 50,000+ daily parallel tests using multi-region hub architecture to reduce latency and improve fault tolerance. E-commerce platforms leverage Grid for Black Friday readiness testing, simulating thousands of concurrent users across Chrome, Firefox, Safari, and mobile browsers. CI/CD pipelines integrate Grid for comprehensive cross-browser validation before deployments.

## Code Example
```java
// Grid configuration for large-scale testing
DesiredCapabilities caps = new DesiredCapabilities();
caps.setBrowserName("chrome");
caps.setPlatform(Platform.WINDOWS_10);
caps.setVersion("latest");

// Connect to load-balanced hub
URL hubUrl = new URL("https://grid.company.com:4444/wd/hub");
WebDriver driver = new RemoteWebDriver(hubUrl, caps);

// Configure session timeout and retry logic
driver.manage().timeouts().pageLoadTimeout(30, TimeUnit.SECONDS);
```

## Performance Optimization
Implement session pooling with pre-warmed browsers, set aggressive 300-second idle timeouts, and monitor memory usage (1.5GB per session). Use health checks every 30 seconds to detect zombie nodes, and configure auto-scaling based on queue depth. Always call driver.quit() instead of driver.close() to ensure proper cleanup and prevent memory leaks.

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-time streaming paths, backpressure management, reconnection handling, and cross-service visibility in a realistic production-like setup.

## Key Concepts
- Real-time data ingestion over WebSocket
- Normalization to canonical schema
- Downstream fan-out via message bus with backpressure
- Burst load simulation, tick miss detection, and reconnection handling
- End-to-end latency SLA verification and audit logging

## Code Example
```javascript
// Synthetic tick generator
const ws = new WebSocket('wss://tdp.example');
ws.onopen = () => {
  for (let i = 0; i < 100000; i++) {
    ws.send(JSON.stringify({ symbol: 'AAPL', price: 150 + Math.random(), ts: Date.now() }));
  }
};

// Verify end-to-end latency
const tickSent = Date.now();
ws.onmessage = (msg) => {
  const latency = Date.now() - JSON.parse(msg.data).sentAt;
  if (latency > SLA_THRESHOLD_MS) alert(`SLA breach: ${latency}ms`);
};

// Reconnection with missed-tick detection
ws.onclose = () => reconnect(lastSequenceId);
```

